  │ [38;5;153mgetUserId()[39m     │ [38;5;153mstring[39m | [38;5;153mnull[39m                  │ [38;5;153mnull[39m whenever consent isn't granted              │
  ├─────────────────┼────────────────────────────────┼──────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
  │ [38;5;153mgrantConsent()[39m  │ [38;5;153mstring[39m                         │ Mints the id if absent, returns it               │
  ├─────────────────┼────────────────────────────────┼──────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
  │ [38;5;153mrevokeConsent()[39m │ [38;5;153mvoid[39m                           │ Records [38;5;153m"denied"[39m [1mand[0m deletes the id              │
  ├─────────────────┼────────────────────────────────┼──────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
  │ [38;5;153mclear()[39m         │ [38;5;153mvoid[39m                           │ Removes the record entirely, back to [38;5;153m"unset"[39m     │
  └─────────────────┴────────────────────────────────┴──────────────────────────────────────────────────┘

  Consent is tri-state, not a boolean, because "hasn't been asked" and "said no" need different behavior — the first
  shows the banner, the second must not nag.

  [1mData model[0m — one [38;5;153mlocalStorage[39m key, [38;5;153mapp.identity[39m, holding one versioned JSON object:

  { [36m"v"[39m: [32m1[39m, [36m"consent"[39m: [31m"granted"[39m, [36m"userId"[39m: [31m"…"[39m, [36m"createdAt"[39m: [31m"2026-09-16T…Z"[39m }

  Single key rather than separate [38;5;153mconsent[39m and [38;5;153muserId[39m keys: two keys can desync into "consent revoked, id still present,"
  which is precisely the state a consent gate exists to prevent. [38;5;153mv[39m gives one place to handle format changes when
  server-issued ids eventually arrive.

  [1mId generation:[0m [38;5;153mcrypto.randomUUID()[39m when available, falling back to building a v4 UUID from [38;5;153mcrypto.getRandomValues()[39m.
  [38;5;153mrandomUUID[39m is restricted to secure contexts; [38;5;153mgetRandomValues[39m is not, so the fallback keeps the page working when
  opened straight from disk. No [38;5;153mMath.random()[39m anywhere — it's not suitable for identifiers.

  [1mFailure handling[0m, the part most likely to bite:
  - [38;5;153mlocalStorage[39m throws in Safari private browsing and when storage is disabled. Every access is wrapped; on failure the
    module degrades to an in-memory record for the page's lifetime. The app keeps working, the id just doesn't survive
    reload.
  - A corrupt or unknown-[38;5;153mv[39m record is treated as [38;5;153m"unset"[39m rather than throwing — a bad blob shouldn't brick the login
    form.
  - [38;5;153mgrantConsent()[39m is idempotent: called twice, the second call returns the existing id rather than minting a new one.

  Does this section look right before I move on to the banner and the [38;5;153mlogin[39m integration?

